%% One fault end to end, SIMPLIFIED for the report (Figure 3.4). The detailed version, with
%% every endpoint, transition and background worker, is e2e-sequence-full.mmd.

sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor R as Reporter<br/>(Flutter)
    actor M as Manager<br/>(React)
    actor T as Technician<br/>(Flutter)
    participant API as ASP.NET Core API
    participant DB as PostgreSQL
    participant AG as Agent service

    Note over R,AG: 1 · Report and clarification
    R->>API: Report a fault (description, room, scanned asset)
    API->>DB: Save report + workflow
    API-->>R: 201 Created (does not wait for the agents)
    API->>AG: Run agents in the background
    AG->>API: Tool calls: room, asset
    AG-->>API: Plan + up to 2 questions
    API->>DB: Save plan, steps and questions
    Note over R,API: Human pause 1: waiting on the reporter
    R->>API: Answer the questions (one form)

    Note over R,AG: 2 · Diagnosis and proposal
    API->>AG: Resume with the answers
    AG->>API: Tool calls: service history, open work orders
    AG-->>API: Diagnosis + proposed repair and cost
    API->>API: Approval gate (C#): over Rs 15,000 or a replacement?
    API->>DB: Work order waiting for approval

    Note over M,API: 3 · Human pause 2: approval
    M->>API: Approve (or reject / request revision)
    M->>API: Assign a technician, book a free slot
    API->>DB: Approved and scheduled

    Note over T,DB: 4 · Repair
    T->>API: Complete the job (outcome, cost, note, photo)
    API->>DB: One transaction: order completed, service record, verification check

    Note over R,AG: 5 · Verification, 5 days later
    R->>API: Is the problem fixed? → No, still broken
    API->>DB: Reopen the workflow
    API->>AG: Diagnose again (now sees the failed repair)
    API->>AG: Verification agent reviews the repair (advice only)
